+`distinct type meters = f32;` is a new type with `f32`'s layout. Nothing converts implicitly: `cast<f32>(m)` and `meters{ f32 }` (`cast<meters>(x)`) convert. It gets no operators unless it asks with `@std::derive::arithmetic` (meters + meters) or writes them.
+
+### Library error frames
+`@std::diagnostic_frame{ .hide = true }` on a template of a library drops its frames from instantiation chains in errors, so a user sees the call in their code and the check that failed, not the library's internals.
+
 ## Rationale
 - **Declared parameters:** every name in a declaration is introduced somewhere a reader can see. `template<type T>` is familiar from C++, and `type` is shorter than `typename`.
 - **Instantiation at compile time:** a file using `std::darray<employee>` needs its size immediately to put it on the stack, and `deduce` needs the return type to type-check the caller. Only the *duplicate machine code* can wait until link time. The header/source split is still kept, because the body travels in the compiled interface.
 - **Interfaces as constraints:** one concept (interface) serves both dynamic dispatch and generic constraints, so there's nothing extra like C++ "concepts" to learn.
 - **`if compile_time` instead of specialization:** all variants are in one place and can be read top to bottom.
+- **Associated types and retroactive implementations:** these are what generic libraries the size of Boost need (graphs, geometry, ranges). The orphan rule (`implement` only next to the interface or the type) keeps them coherent.
+- **Distinct types:** units and ids are where C++ code mixes up `float`s. A distinct type costs nothing at run time and turns the mix-up into a compile error.
diff --git a/docs/design/language/functions/generators.md b/docs/design/language/functions/generators.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1a7cd6b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/design/language/functions/generators.md
@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+# Generators and Resumable Functions
+
+A **generator** produces a sequence of values, one per `yield`, and keeps its state between them. A **resumable function** can stop at an `await` and continue later. Both are ordinary values with a size known at compile time: they never allocate unless the program says so.
+
+## Rules
+
+### 1. Generators
+```edax
+generator<u32> evens(u32 limit) {
+    for (u32 i : 0..limit) {
+        if (i % 2 == 0) {
+            yield i;
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+for (u32 n : evens(10)) {
+    std::println(f"{n}");
+}
+```
+- `generator<T>` as the return type makes the function a generator. `yield value;` hands out one value; reaching the end (or `return;`) ends the sequence.
+- Calling it runs nothing: it returns a **value of an unnamed type** that holds the function's state (its parameters and the locals alive across a `yield`) inline. Like a lambda, it is stored with `auto` or passed to a template.
+- The value is a stream: `next() mut` returns `std::option<T>`, and `for` walks it.
+- A generator with reference parameters or view captures is a **view** (see [Ownership](../memory/ownership-and-references.md)): it can't outlive what it borrows.
+- `std::boxed_generator<T>` erases the type and puts the state on the heap (one visible allocation), for storing generators of different functions in one container.
+- A generator that calls itself needs a box: its state would contain itself. The error says so.
+
+### 2. Resumable functions
+```edax
+resumable<texture> load_texture(std::filesystem::path p, mut io::reader& r) {
+    std::darray<u8> bytes = await r.read_all(p);       // stops here until the read is done
+    return texture::decode(bytes);
+}
+```
+- `resumable<T>` as the return type, and `await x` where `x` implements `std::awaitable<U>`:
+  ```edax
+  interface awaitable<U> {
+      std::option<U> poll(mut std::waker& w) mut;     // none: not ready; call w.wake() when it is
+  }
+  ```
+- Like a generator, calling it returns a value holding its state inline, and it does nothing until polled. A `resumable<T>` value is itself an `awaitable<T>`.
+- **No built-in executor.** The game loop, a thread pool or a script scheduler polls resumables. Nothing runs that the program didn't call.
+- `await` is allowed only in resumable functions. Locals that live across an `await` follow the same rules as captures of a stored lambda: a reference held across an `await` makes the resumable a view.
+
+### 3. Fibers
+Fibers (stackful tasks for job systems) are library code on one intrinsic:
+- `std::raw::stack_switch(mut from, to)` (`unsafe`) saves the registers into `from` and continues on `to`'s stack. Stacks come from an allocator with a guard page.
+- Functions that may switch fibers are marked `@std::may_switch_fiber`; the compiler doesn't keep a `thread_local` address in a register across a call to them. The library's job system marks its wait functions.
+
+## Rationale
+- **State inline, size known:** C++ coroutines allocate their frame on the heap unless the optimizer removes it. In Edax the frame is part of the value, like a lambda's captures, so the cost is visible and usually zero.
+- **Borrow rules for frames:** a generator that borrows is a view, so a frame can't outlive what it points to. That is the classic dangling-coroutine bug of C++.
+- **No executor in the language:** engines schedule work their own way (per frame, per job system, per script). A built-in runtime would be a hidden cost and a second way to do it.
+- **Generators replace iterator classes:** most hand-written iterators are a loop with a cursor. A generator is that loop, written once.
diff --git a/docs/design/language/functions/lambdas.md b/docs/design/language/functions/lambdas.md
index 42d9aef..729f69d 100755
--- a/docs/design/language/functions/lambdas.md
+++ b/docs/design/language/functions/lambdas.md
@@ -39,6 +39,17 @@ employees.retain([](employee& e) { return e.age >= 30; });
 - Lambdas with no captures convert to `std::fn_ptr<R(Args...)>`, a plain function pointer for callbacks and FFI.
 - A `std::function` owns its callable: it is moved, never copied or cloned. A field of a callable type is called like a method: `this.on_click(n)`.
 
+### Changing captured values
+- `[mut count = 0u32]` (or `[mut move state]`) declares a captured value that the lambda may change. Such a lambda's call is `mut`: it is called through a `mut` variable, a `mut` reference, or a `std::function` that is itself `mut`.
+
+### Callables with bounds
+- `std::function<R(A...), I...>` stores a callable that implements the interfaces `I...` and implements them itself: `std::function<void(), std::sendable>` can be moved to another thread. Type erasure with bounds is general: `std::any_of<I...>` holds a value of any type implementing `I...`.
+- `std::function` takes an allocator like the containers.
+- `std::inline_function<R(A...), N>` stores captures of up to `N` bytes inline and never allocates. A lambda with bigger captures is a compile error that names its size.
+
 ## Rationale
 - **`[mut &x]`:** it uses the same `mut` marker as references and call sites, so a lambda that changes a variable says so in its capture list.
 - **Reference-capturing lambdas are views:** this closes the classic C++ dangling-capture bug (returning `[&]` lambdas) without any lifetime syntax.
+- **`mut` captures:** a handler with its own counter or cache is common; marking the call `mut` keeps "nothing changes behind an immutable reference" true.
+- **Bounds on `std::function`:** an event bus or job queue that moves callables between threads needs them to be sendable, and the type must say so.
+- **`inline_function`:** per-frame callbacks shouldn't allocate. The size limit is in the type, so the cost is visible.
